00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.410
So let me highlight
the values that we

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:15.780
learned throughout this
experience very quickly.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.450
First, Container should help
improve your productivity

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.070
by packaging up--

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.030
you can package up your
favorite tools and frameworks

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.160
into container images.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.880
Then you can share
them across the teams

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.300
to standardize the
tools and the frameworks

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.680
and all the, perhaps, workflows.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.100
This will basically allow
you to reuse everything

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.890
that somebody has
created and then

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.440
prevent reinventing
the wheels, which

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.620
is great for productivity.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.230
Secondly, Container
will also help

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:57.060
in reproducing reliable results.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.900
This is very important
for data scientists.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:03.180
Anyone using the same image
with the same configuration

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.810
pre-configured and with the
same tools and frameworks

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.090
and the same data,
technically, everyone

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.710
should be able to reproduce
very reliable results.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.790
Instead of someone did
it in a different way.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:19.380
And as a result, you have to
figure out why that happened.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.940
It's very time consuming.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.020
But through standardizing,
using the same thing,

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.420
it will give you some--

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.020
reproducing the
reliable results.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.890
Third one is portability.

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.140
You can easily pour your
environment from one

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.350
to another with Containers.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.990
We can also take
Containers very quickly

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:45.370
to the Cloud to take advantage
of the unlimited, huge

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.070
resources whenever you need.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.970
So now we briefly understand
the value, how Containers

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.540
are great for Machine Learning.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.160
Is that it?

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.420
Are we good enough?

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.300
Let's step back for a moment
about the overall picture.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.330
The reason that we're
investing Machine Learning

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:13.340
could be improving greater
or providing greater user

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.830
experience through
Machine Learning

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.200
or perhaps increasing
productivity

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.840
through the automation that
Machine Learning provides.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.180
So the role here is to--

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:27.050
in this diagram-- is to
really get into production

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.860
from research and
training as quickly

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.970
as possible to maximize
such investments.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:40.910
For that, we need to train
their models at a large scale

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.080
and then deploy the
trained models continuously

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.650
and quick as possible in
production for inference.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:53.720
This implies that for
your Containers, which

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.810
has your Machine
Learning applications,

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:59.900
we need an infrastructure to
actually get to this point.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.870
And that's exactly where
Kubernetes come into play.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.600
So let's take a look into
the values of Kubernetes.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.540
First is scales.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.540
Kubernetes provides very
scalable infrastructure

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:19.240
to process massive, massive
number of workloads.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.020
And also, you can
also run Kubernetes

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.210
on a single workstation.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:27.230
And you can also run it on
thousands of nodes in Cloud

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:28.490
as well.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.010
So it scales very well,
and it provides you

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:32.535
a scalable infrastructure.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.910
Kubernetes would also help you
increase your productivity.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.580
What I mean by that
is it will free you up

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:48.980
from managing your own
workstation, servers, even VMs.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:54.490
It lets you just simply focus on
your application and services.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.274
All the things that
you have underneath it

00:06:57.274 --> 00:06:59.440
is something that you want
to let Kubernetes handle.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.500
And lastly, your Kubernetes
applications are portable.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.320
You could pour your workloads
to literally any Kubernetes

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.230
deployment, whether it's
an On-premise, or On-prem,

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:16.770
or in Cloud.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.630
Kubernetes provides an
open and standard API,

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:23.880
and it's portable across
various products and services--

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.070
really across vendors.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:29.280
And that vendor neutrality
is defined open source.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.240
This portability is very,
very important for us--

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.753
I mean, not only for us,
but for the community.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.240
So let me quickly
wrap up the values

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.540
that we just described,
why Containers

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:52.980
and Kubernetes for Machine
Learning with this diagram.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.330
So basically, let's call this
a Cloud native Machine Learning

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.360
stack.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.860
In a nutshell, you just choose
your favorite Machine Learning

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:07.230
framework and tools and
pack it up into Containers

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:12.040
and then run them on
Kubernetes at scale.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.670
It helps you to be productive.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:18.960
It will help you to
reproduce reliable results.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.590
It will also help you to pour
your application wherever

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:27.600
you like, either it's
an On-prem or in Cloud.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.580
It also scales from a single
workstation in front of you

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.140
to thousands and thousands
of nodes in Cloud.

