memory, about 16 gigs.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:54.462
And I also allocated
a single GPU rate.

00:14:54.462 --> 00:14:55.920
I didn't have to
manually allocate.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.050
But then the whole Minikube
workflow automatically

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.130
picks it up.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.610
So we have a Minikube
with a GPU attached.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:06.540
So next off, our
next part of our demo

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.660
is to actually show
an ML training job

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.177
and show it to be running
on Minikube locally.

00:15:12.177 --> 00:15:14.510
And then let's see if we can
pull up TensorFlow and just

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:17.015
show us the jobs progress.

00:15:17.015 --> 00:15:18.390
For that, what
I'm am going to do

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.070
is I'm going to use
a pre-canned model

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:23.460
because the point of this demo
is not to build cool ML models,

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:25.680
but rather show how
infrastructure just

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.370
works seamlessly.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:29.580
So I'm going to
use a pre-trained

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:33.232
or a pre-built model, which is
ResNet, for those of you who

00:15:33.232 --> 00:15:34.190
are familiar with that.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:37.230
I'm using ResNet-50
specifically in this case.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.730
Google TensorFlow
team has already

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.220
optimized that quite a bit.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:41.990
So I'm going to
reuse the same model.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.510
And I have it packaged as
part of a Container image.

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:50.320
And I have my data set for this
model stored on my workstation.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.210
So I'm going to use an FS to map
the data set into my Minikube

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.720
VM, and similarly I cam map and
make code, also, from a host.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:02.400
So both are possibilities that
you have working iteratively

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:03.790
on your workstation.

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:08.670
So let me go ahead
and get to that.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:17.440
So I'm going to go in
and launch the job here.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.050
So for those of you
who are curious,

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:22.450
let me show you what
job actually launched.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:24.390
I'm pulling it up on
GitHub because it's

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:26.170
a little bit easier to read.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:30.240
So I have a container here that
has TensorFlow under ResNet

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:31.610
repository that I showed--

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.990
already pre-packaged
as part of Container.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:37.560
And I'm running literally
the same instructions

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:40.284
that's mentioned on the
official ResNet model, right?

00:16:40.284 --> 00:16:41.700
It's the same set
of instructions,

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:43.650
except that it's
run from a Container

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:47.354
and it's declaratively specified
as a Kubernetes configuration.

00:16:47.354 --> 00:16:49.770
In this configuration, you can
take and port it and run it

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:51.850
on any Minikube and you
will get the same results.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.308
And that's what
Yoshi was referring

00:16:53.308 --> 00:16:58.322
to when he said portability
and reproducibility.

00:16:58.322 --> 00:17:00.030
And I also tweaked
some of the parameters

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.000
to make sure that it
runs reasonably locally.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.700
I don't want to wait for 10
days for one [INAUDIBLE] to run.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.900
So I want it to be really fast.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.930
So now that's done,
let's go look at the job,

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.250
if it's actually running.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:14.040
So the job is already running.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:21.839
Let's look at its logs and
see if it's showing GPUs.

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:23.880
So what we are
expecting to see is

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:27.240
we're expecting TensorFlow
to show that it's using GPU.

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:33.960
So we can see that
here, it's showing

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.850
that it's using a GPU device--

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:36.940
Quadro M4000.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:40.030
That happens to be the free
GPU on the workstation,

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:42.390
and TensorFlow is
automatically picking that up.

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:43.670
And you didn't have
to change much, right?

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.503
You didn't really have
to change TensorFlow.

00:17:45.503 --> 00:17:48.180
You could take a pre-canned
model and run it on Kubernetes.

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.100
And training is
already starting.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.040
And if you were to pull
up TensorBoard for this,

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.570
it would look
something like this.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.290
So training is happening
now, and if you

00:17:58.290 --> 00:17:59.690
look at the
TensorBoard instance,

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.170
this is a pre-trial run
that I did before the demo.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:04.920
But you've seen that
if we wait for a day,

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.650
it'll make some progress off
the board, like two [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:11.180
and the progress rate is
about two images per second.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:14.010
So this is not really
for real training,

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.810
but this is more for
fine-tuning and getting a model

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:19.260
working so you can
actually do it at scale

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:20.970
on really large machines.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:25.100
So that's sort of all I had
to say about Minikube, Yoshi.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.100
YOSHI TAMURA: All right.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:26.810
Thanks, Vish.

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:28.687
VISHNU KANNAN: Want to
switch back to slides.

00:18:28.687 --> 00:18:29.770
YOSHI TAMURA: That's good.

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.410
All righty.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:32.270
Thanks, Vish.

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:35.700
So now we have our first
ML application running

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:37.410
on Minikube, and it's great.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.480
And it's using GPU.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:44.010
Now luckily, we are already
saturating the GPU there

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.130
and we saw their
application remodels

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.430
are heavier than we thought.

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:49.970
So what should we do?

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:53.350
Should we buy more
workstations or should we

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:57.100
build a data center or at least
plan building a data center?

00:18:57.100 --> 00:18:58.630
Probably not.

00:18:58.630 --> 00:19:01.450
Let's go to Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:03.880
because it's the
best place to run

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.760
Machine Learning in Containers.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.570
That's because GPUs
in Kubernetes Engine

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:16.000
is now generally available
and ready for the enterprise.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.530
GPUs in Kubernetes Engine
allows your Container

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:24.160
to access GPU via
Kubernetes API.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:28.450
There's no need to manage your
own hardware, like workstation,

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:30.370
servers, or even VMs.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:34.330
Just consume the GPUs
whenever you need

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:36.280
and as much as you like.

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:40.990
This flexibility is something
that Cloud can provide.

00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:44.020
And we're also excited
that our customers

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:45.790
are loving this product.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:51.070
As the GPU core hours, since
we introduced [INAUDIBLE] back

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:57.190
in 2017, the core hours has
exploded to more than 10x.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:19:59.770
And this is very,
very exciting for us,

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:02.650
and whenever we have this
conversation about, and getting

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:04.270
feedback about,
this feature, people

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:08.950
are just loving the
elastic, flexible aspect

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:11.080
of accessing GPUs
through Kubernetes

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.672
Engine for their Containers.

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:21.180
We have a wide range
of GPUs in Kubernetes.

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:24.840
All GPU models available
in Compute Engine

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:29.310
can be also used in
Kubernetes Engine.

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.670
For example, NVIDIA V100--

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.700
this is the high-end
GPU with top performance

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.930
for both training and inference.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:40.510
So if you want to do
training and inference,

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:45.480
this V100 is a perfect choice
because it's good at both.

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:48.150
P100 is a well-balanced GPU--

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:51.990
good for performance and
the cost is a lot lower.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:55.890
That balance is something
that people like about it.

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.700
K80, the last, but
really not the least

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:03.150
because this is, indeed,
the most widely used GPU,

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:04.560
as far as we are aware of.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.882
And it's great for
starters, so if you're

00:21:06.882 --> 00:21:08.340
done with a
workstation-- if you're

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:10.590
wondering which GPU to
use, K80 is definitely

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:13.660
a good starting point.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:17.190
So speaking of reducing cost,
I didn't mention in the slide,

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:20.740
but we also have an interesting
feature called preemptible VMs

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.850
or preemptible GPUs,
where you can access

00:21:23.850 --> 00:21:26.370
this GPU with a lot lower cost.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:29.220
The difference is
that your GPU could

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:32.370
be evicted with a
short notice, which

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:36.300
means that if you could
tune your application

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.510
to handle checkpoint
frequently or somehow handle

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.670
the robustness part,
you can actually

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:46.020
achieve very high performance
with very low cost.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.960
And some people are
really using this widely

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.610
for their bash workflows.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.070
If you're running
a bash workflow,

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:55.990
you don't actually use
the GPU all the time.

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:59.190
So you just quickly ramp
up and quickly ramp down.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:03.900
And preemptible GPUs are really
a perfect solution for that.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:07.620
Now in addition to these
GPUs that I introduced,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:11.520
we're very excited to announce
that NVIDIA P4 is also

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:15.780
now available as beta
in Kubernetes Engine.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:19.530
P4 is a very unique GPU,
which comes with a very

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:20.655
fast inference performance.

00:22:20.655 --> 00:22:22.321
We didn't talk about
inference, so we're

00:22:22.321 --> 00:22:23.420
going to show that later.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:27.150
But with a very, very fast
inference performance,

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:31.320
but you can actually save
the costs significantly.

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:35.160
So this GPU is a
very unique one,

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:38.486
if you compare it to the
rest of the GPU models

00:22:38.486 --> 00:22:39.610
that we have in our family.

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:40.560
So a great addition.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.960
So let's look into
how you could actually

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:50.020
use GPUs in Kubernetes Engine.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:54.896
In this example, I'm creating
a node pool with GPUs

00:22:54.896 --> 00:22:56.020
and attaching to a cluster.

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:57.670
Node pool is a
very simple concept

00:22:57.670 --> 00:23:00.880
that there's virtual
machines inside a pool,

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.490
and then we specify a GPU there.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:07.030
And you can attach it to any
Kubernetes Engine cluster

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:08.290
whenever you like.

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:11.560
We chose this example because it
will give you more flexibility.

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:14.530
If you wanted to attach
to your existing cluster,

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:15.680
it is more flexible.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.430
And if you don't need
it, you can actually

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.330
scale down the node
pool to 0 later,

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.850
which I will describe later.

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:26.380
In this case, we're creating
two nodes with one NVIDIA--

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:30.440
the top, high-end performance
V100 on each node.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.844
And then attaching to this
pool into an existing cluster.

00:23:34.844 --> 00:23:36.510
And that's pretty
much it for the setup.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:42.350
So now, let's
consume those GPUs.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:46.820
Again, you might realize
that this example is actually

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:51.170
the same as the previous one
that we used for Minikube,

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.810
and you're right.

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:58.411
The same spec-- this one is
requesting two NVIDIA GPUs,

00:23:58.411 --> 00:23:58.910
right?

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:01.340
But this time,
instead of requesting

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:05.120
that on a workstation, it's
requesting two NVIDIA GPUs

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:07.410
in the cluster in the Cloud.

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:12.110
So I hope this will tell you
that Kubernetes API and also

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:16.160
applications are
really, really portable.

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:21.740
GPUs in Kubernetes Engines are
already flexible and scalable,

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.310
but that's not it.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.630
We also have Cluster
Autoscaler with GPU,

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:32.680
and this is really only
available on Google Kubernetes

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:34.620
Engine.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:38.410
It would automatically
scale up the cluster

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:47.020
whenever needed and down to hit
the best performance over cost.

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:52.360
The nodes with the GPUs will
be automatically created

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.550
when a cluster may
need more capacity.

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:58.660
And I think scaling up
is pretty intuitive,

00:24:58.660 --> 00:25:02.650
but because GPUs are much
more expensive, compared

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:06.710
to CPUs, the scaling down aspect
kind of resonates, at least

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:07.990
to me, very well.

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:12.850
So the nodes with GPUs also
get deleted when they're idle,

00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:15.080
so you don't necessarily
have to spin up

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:20.405
a node with empty cycles.

00:25:20.405 --> 00:25:22.280
It will always be
adjusting to your workload.

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:29.310
So then how can we access--

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:31.180
use this cool feature?

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:33.550
It's very simple and easy.

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:37.470
All you have to do is to
turn on the autoscaler

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:41.940
with several flags, and I'm
using the previous example

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.000
here.

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:46.870
So what I did here
is put this enable--

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:51.030
dash, dash, enable
autoscaling, and put--

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:53.190
min is specified.

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:56.640
Going down to scale down
to minimum to 0 node

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:58.680
and scale up to maximum 5 nodes.

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:02.340
So if I need more capacity,
it will go up to 5 nodes.

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:04.315
If I'm idle, it
will go down to 0.

00:26:04.315 --> 00:26:05.690
And this is exactly
why we wanted

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.460
to introduce this node
pool, because if you just

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.640
specify this particular
group to use GPU

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:15.650
and if you don't have any
workload that requires a GPU,

00:26:15.650 --> 00:26:17.040
it can go down to 0.

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.520
And once you have it, it will
scale up back to 5 again.

00:26:23.696 --> 00:26:26.240
Yeah, and the other part
is exactly the same.

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:33.246
We have two nodes in a node
pool and each node has one V100.

00:26:33.246 --> 00:26:36.930
OK, so let's look into
the demonstration again.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.680
So this time, we're going to
go through the demonstration

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:43.520
of GPUs in Kubernetes
Engine, and this time, we're

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:46.650
going to do the training
to show the power of that.

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:47.650
Right back to you, Vish.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:49.430
VISHNU KANNAN: Yeah,
thank you, Yoshi.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:52.640
So I want to show
that it's actually

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.204
really straightforward to do
what Yoshi said through the UI.

00:26:55.204 --> 00:26:57.120
You can do this through
the command line, too.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:58.828
So I won't take too
much time, but I just

00:26:58.828 --> 00:27:01.580
want to highlight the fact that
you can create a cluster pretty

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:05.450
quickly and you can either
attach GPUs to your cluster

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.300
directly, like your
default node pool,

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:11.420
or you can create a
cluster without GPUs

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:14.990
and then add more node pools,
which are showing just a bit.

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:19.040
So I have a pre-created
cluster here

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:21.290
and I can go and edit
this pre-created cluster,

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:23.060
and I can add node pools.

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.240
And it's not just this.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.740
There will be another
talk later today--

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:28.870
or is it today?

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:30.620
YOSHI TAMURA: I think
I have that one in--

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:31.050
VISHNU KANNAN: OK, awesome.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:32.883
So Yoshi will talk about
that in just a bit.

00:27:32.883 --> 00:27:34.457
But there, GKE
would automatically

00:27:34.457 --> 00:27:35.540
create node pools for you.

00:27:35.540 --> 00:27:38.570
So you don't even have to go
manually create GPU node pools.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:40.900
So for the purpose
of this demo, I

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:44.034
have pre-created a
GKE cluster and I have

00:27:44.034 --> 00:27:45.200
various kinds of node pools.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:46.310
The default one is
the one that you

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:48.170
get when you create
the cluster, and then I

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:52.970
have a GPU pool where each
node has one GPU attached.

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:56.420
So I have 10 nodes
and I have 10 GPUs.

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.280
And autoscaling is
turned on, where

00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:00.840
I can scale from 10 to 100.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.440
And I have another
node pool where

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:07.640
each node has 8 [INAUDIBLE]
that's attached to it,

