you definitely do want to have
your existing version running,

00:39:07.990 --> 00:39:10.330
and then keep updating
behind the scene.

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:13.030
And that's where Kubernetes
is really good at--

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:16.610
and with this GPU integration,
it's all coming together.

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:18.470
So I think this
kind of shows you

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:20.560
how Kubernetes and
Kubernetes Engine

00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:22.390
helps you to bring
your ML application

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:25.300
from training to production
in a very light way.

00:39:25.300 --> 00:39:28.720
We didn't even cover the
hot, CICD tool chain.

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:30.780
He just edited online.

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:33.122
I'm sure that that's not
the best way, apparently.

00:39:33.122 --> 00:39:34.830
So we should definitely
include some sort

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:38.800
of a version controlling
assistant into the workflow.

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:42.010
But I want to say that that's
the kind of [INAUDIBLE] that we

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:45.950
do have on Kubernetes
Engines with GPUs.

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:47.890
So I introduced that
GPUs and Kubernetes

00:39:47.890 --> 00:39:49.084
are ready for Enterprise.

00:39:49.084 --> 00:39:51.500
And if I just say so, it's
like, yeah, you're saying that.

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:55.450
But we would like to share
some voices from our users.

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:59.610
Ocado, the world's largest
online only grocery retailer,

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:02.760
has been using our GPUs
in Kubernetes Engine.

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:05.520
They said, GPUs in
Kubernetes Engines

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:08.730
are powerful, cost
effective, and

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:11.730
flexible for their
enterprise-grade machine

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:12.990
learning.

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:19.380
We're very excited about their
customers loving our product.

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:23.400
So GPUs and Kubernetes Engines
are perfect for your ML

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:26.370
application, and we
hopefully highlighted

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:28.260
the key parts of that.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:34.690
However, machine learning, as we
also saw from today's keynote--

00:40:34.690 --> 00:40:37.490
it is evolving
every day, really.

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:42.410
And the challenge there is
this increased complexity.

00:40:42.410 --> 00:40:44.980
And as a result,
the compute needs.

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:49.160
What if you need more
power in the future?

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:54.110
We at Google hit that
problem five years ago.

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:57.770
If people use voice search
with speed recognition

00:40:57.770 --> 00:41:00.740
for just three minutes a day,
it's like five years ago--

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:01.680
a long time.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:05.210
So I'm pretty sure, with all the
cool gadgets like Google Home

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:08.960
and Assistant, I think this
number is too pessimistic

00:41:08.960 --> 00:41:09.890
nowadays.

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:13.625
But they said we would need to
double our data center-- double

00:41:13.625 --> 00:41:15.542
our data center, meaning
Google data centers--

00:41:15.542 --> 00:41:16.833
they had a ton of data centers.

00:41:16.833 --> 00:41:19.280
But they said, we've got to
double to meet the computation

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:20.150
demands.

00:41:20.150 --> 00:41:23.060
This really, really big moment.

00:41:23.060 --> 00:41:27.710
And this is exactly why
we build Tensor processing

00:41:27.710 --> 00:41:29.330
unit or short--

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:31.220
TPU.

00:41:31.220 --> 00:41:33.230
Today, we're very
excited to announce

00:41:33.230 --> 00:41:37.340
that this Cloud
TPU in Kubernetes

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:41.240
is now publicly
available in beta.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:44.000
You can now use
hardware accelerator

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.640
that Google designed
just for machine

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:48.830
learning in Kubernetes Engine.

00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:53.810
Like we have been
consistent, you

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:57.980
can access this Cloud
TPU via Kubernetes API,

00:41:57.980 --> 00:42:00.350
like many other resources.

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:04.010
You don't need to manage
the cloud TPU by yourself.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:08.360
Kubernetes engine will take
care of the lifecycle for you.

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:12.530
It would also automatically
scale the number of cloud TPUs

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:14.312
whenever you need
and as much as you

00:42:14.312 --> 00:42:15.770
like, as far as
you have the quota.

00:42:19.190 --> 00:42:23.690
The performance of Cloud
TPU is just unbelievable.

00:42:23.690 --> 00:42:28.370
On a single Cloud TPU
device with eight TPU cores,

00:42:28.370 --> 00:42:30.380
you can train the
ResNet-50 that Vish

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:36.300
has been showing with more
than 3,000 images per second.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:41.020
And it finishes in
about nine hours.

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:47.100
Not only that-- with
this half-size TPU pod,

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:52.110
you can train with more than
77,000 images per second.

00:42:52.110 --> 00:42:54.960
Honestly, I'm not
a person who runs

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:58.270
Machine Learning all the time
or models also all the time.

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:00.420
So this number is kind
of too large, really.

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:03.720
Too hard to kind of feel
what this really means.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:06.750
So just telling you that,
OK, I mentioned nine hours

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:09.690
with that one Cloud TPU device.

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:15.150
This gigantic thing will get
the job done in 30 minutes,

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:17.970
and I think that's
much more tangible.

00:43:17.970 --> 00:43:23.110
So with the time that you
reduce, you can take a break

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:25.980
and go home early or,
more seriously, I think,

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:28.980
you can spend more time
on interesting stuff.

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:31.780
You can make this cycle
faster and faster.

00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:34.000
So this performance
is just mind blowing.

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:41.180
And I think you kind of imagine
access into this Cloud TPU

00:43:41.180 --> 00:43:44.780
in Kubernetes Engine is
very straightforward.

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:48.400
When creating a cluster, all you
have to do is to specify in it

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:50.570
dash, dash, enable TPU.

00:43:50.570 --> 00:43:53.720
We recently introduced
this feature from 110,

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:57.680
so we just wanted to specify
or convey that clearly.

00:43:57.680 --> 00:44:00.590
But eventually, when the
Google Kubernetes always

00:44:00.590 --> 00:44:04.400
comes with larger than 110, you
don't need this flag anymore.

00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:08.920
And we're using IP Alias
in the behind the scenes,

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:12.510
so we're putting dash, dash,
enable IP Alias to enable it.

00:44:12.510 --> 00:44:15.020
But that's pretty
much it for the setup.

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:23.510
So now, let's consume the
Cloud TPUs for our containers.

00:44:23.510 --> 00:44:27.580
This time, we cannot use the
same example that I've been

00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:31.060
using here and there
because instead of GPUs,

00:44:31.060 --> 00:44:32.710
we're requesting Cloud TPUs.

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:35.300
So we have to change that.

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:40.420
But in this case, we're
requesting eight cloud TPU V2

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:43.460
cores, which is
actually one cloud TPU

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:45.940
device from our quota.

00:44:45.940 --> 00:44:50.100
And that's the change you have
to make for your prospect.

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:51.020
All right.

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:55.810
Then let's see how it's working,
and Vish, come on again.

00:44:55.810 --> 00:44:58.357
So how can I use a Cloud
TPU from Kubernetes Engine?

00:44:58.357 --> 00:45:00.440
VISHNU KANNAN: It's actually
very straightforward.

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:02.500
So again, I'm going
to bore everyone

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:04.250
with the same model
and the same prospect.

00:45:04.250 --> 00:45:07.190
There's nothing really fancy
going on this time, either.

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:11.050
So I have the same model
code that I showed earlier,

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:15.920
and we are running a slightly
simpler set of parameters

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:19.410
here because the model is
already setup, by default,

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:22.100
to run on TPUs because
I used a model that

00:45:22.100 --> 00:45:24.380
was already optimized for TPUs.

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:27.170
And that's the thing that I
really like the most with TPUs,

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:29.540
is that you get
these off the shelf

00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:31.910
models that are
already optimized,

00:45:31.910 --> 00:45:35.710
where if your business
case is just to apply ML

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:37.970
to your existing
data, where you only

00:45:37.970 --> 00:45:39.884
make some tweaks to
your existing models,

00:45:39.884 --> 00:45:41.050
it's really straightforward.

00:45:41.050 --> 00:45:43.870
You don't have to fiddle
with anything else,

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:45.520
and you can just get going.

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:47.060
And if you want
to run it at scale

00:45:47.060 --> 00:45:48.950
or if you want to
run it periodically--

00:45:48.950 --> 00:45:51.050
if you want any sort
of automation on that,

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:52.950
GKE makes it really,
really simple.

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:58.310
So as Yoshi said, I requested
a single TPU donut here.

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:02.787
I also specified
TensorFlow version 1.9,

00:46:02.787 --> 00:46:04.370
and this is something
you can do where

00:46:04.370 --> 00:46:08.834
you can choose specific versions
of TensorFlow so that it works.

00:46:08.834 --> 00:46:11.000
What I've done in the
background, just to save time,

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:13.360
is I already went ahead and
started this job that I just

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:13.860
showed.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:17.870
And I want to pull up the
GKE workloads UI again.

00:46:17.870 --> 00:46:21.710
So what we see here
is the ResNet job.

00:46:21.710 --> 00:46:26.120
It's aptly named ResNet
TPU, and there's a part

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:27.360
that belongs to this job.

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:31.560
And if you go and look at
the jobs requirements--

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:33.040
let me pull this up--

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:39.540
you'll see that it's asking
for a single TPU donut.

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:41.190
And training has
already started,

00:46:41.190 --> 00:46:44.800
so you can probably look at
its logs and see that it's--

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:47.270
so see here that training
is already going on.

00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:51.080
It's processing some number
of samples per second.

00:46:51.080 --> 00:46:53.087
I also want to pull
up TensorBoard.

00:46:53.087 --> 00:46:55.670
So I can pull up TensorBoard for
this exact training instance,

00:46:55.670 --> 00:46:57.170
when all I've done
is I've pulled up

00:46:57.170 --> 00:47:01.130
TensorBoard for an instance that
has already been pre-trained.

00:47:01.130 --> 00:47:04.520
And what we see here is we see
that with the default hyper

00:47:04.520 --> 00:47:06.781
parameters that have
been set up for ResNet,

00:47:06.781 --> 00:47:08.780
you are able to process
about-- like Yoshi said,

00:47:08.780 --> 00:47:12.170
you are able to process
about 3,350 or 3,400 images

00:47:12.170 --> 00:47:13.660
per second.

00:47:13.660 --> 00:47:15.920
And the other cool part that
I really like about TPUs

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:18.580
is that if you want to
scale beyond a single donut

00:47:18.580 --> 00:47:20.810
and if you want to
get to larger slices,

00:47:20.810 --> 00:47:23.210
you just increase the bat size.

00:47:23.210 --> 00:47:25.790
I'm not sure for what all models
it works, but for the ones

00:47:25.790 --> 00:47:32.770
that it works, it's a really
easy to use user interface.

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:34.994
That's all I had
about TPUs, Yoshi.

00:47:34.994 --> 00:47:36.410
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank
you very much.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:38.400
That's really, really
impressive and powerful.

00:47:41.142 --> 00:47:42.410
All righty.

00:47:42.410 --> 00:47:47.480
So I think it's time to
wrap up our long journey.

00:47:47.480 --> 00:47:51.540
We introduced a number
of projects and products,

00:47:51.540 --> 00:47:54.410
but all of these are
something that you

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:56.420
could start using today.

00:47:56.420 --> 00:47:58.280
There's no whitelist.

00:47:58.280 --> 00:48:01.040
There's no need to contact
me to-- can I use it?

00:48:01.040 --> 00:48:03.210
Everything is all
available to you.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:07.520
And we hope this talk will be
also online very soon so that--

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:11.660
we cover a lot so you can go
through Vish's demonstration

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:16.440
and really, really get your
hands dirty by yourself.

00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:20.600
So just to kind of recap,
we introduced GPU support

00:48:20.600 --> 00:48:23.630
in Minikube, which is
great for starters.

00:48:23.630 --> 00:48:25.760
We talk about GPUs
in Kubernetes,

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:28.430
which is generally available
with a wide range of GPUs

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.370
available and with
the autoscaler.

00:48:31.370 --> 00:48:33.650
And lastly, we
announced the Cloud TPU

00:48:33.650 --> 00:48:35.540
in Kubernetes Engines.

00:48:35.540 --> 00:48:38.840
But besides all those products
and features, what I really

00:48:38.840 --> 00:48:42.590
wanted to convey today is
that Containers are really

00:48:42.590 --> 00:48:44.420
great for Machine Learning.

00:48:44.420 --> 00:48:46.700
And I could confidently
say that from my experience

00:48:46.700 --> 00:48:50.420
inside of Google and talking to
Vish and all the great Googlers

00:48:50.420 --> 00:48:51.440
inside.

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:55.860
And Kubernetes is indeed great
for search containers, as well.

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:59.510
So going back to
this stack again,

00:48:59.510 --> 00:49:03.600
but choose your favorite ML,
Machine Learning, framework

00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:04.740
and tools.

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:07.370
Pack it up into Containers.

00:49:07.370 --> 00:49:10.130
Run on Kubernetes at scale.

00:49:10.130 --> 00:49:13.260
That's what I really
wanted to talk about today.

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:14.720
So thank you for listening.

